Here's an example Java code snippet that demonstrates how to receive IMAP mail and store the subject and message content in a hash table:

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import javax.mail.Folder;
import javax.mail.Message;
import javax.mail.Part;
import javax.mail.Store;
import javax.mail.internet.InternetAddress;
import javax.mail.internet.MimeMessage;

public class ImapMailReceiver {

    public static Map<String, String> receiveAndStoreMail(String imapServer, String username, String password) {
        Map<String, String> mailData = new HashMap<>();
        Map<String, String> subjectAndMessage = new HashMap<>();

        try {
            // Set up the IMAP store
            Store store = Session.getInstance(new InternetAddress(username), password).getStore("imap");
            store.connect(imapServer);

            // Get the inbox folder
            Folder folder = store.getFolder("INBOX");
            folder.open(Folder.READ_ONLY);

            // Iterate through each message in the inbox
            for (Message message : folder.getMessages()) {
                String subject = message.getSubject();
                String messageContent = getMessageContent(message);

                // Store the subject and message content in the hash table
                subjectAndMessage.put(subject, messageContent);
            }

            // Close the folder and store
            folder.close(false);
            store.close();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }

        return subjectAndMessage;
    }

    private static String getMessageContent(Message message) throws Exception {
        // Check if the message is a MIME message
        if (message instanceof MimeMessage) {
            Part part = ((MimeMessage) message).getContent();
            if (part != null) {
                return part.toString();
            }
        }
        return "";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String imapServer = "imap.example.com";
        String username = "your_username";
        String password = "your_password";

        Map<String, String> mailData = receiveAndStoreMail(imapServer, username, password);
        // Now you can access the subject and message content from the mailData map
    }
}
```

In this code:
- We import necessary classes for handling email and exceptions.
- The `receiveAndStoreMail` method sets up an IMAP connection, opens the inbox folder, and iterates through each message.
- For each message, it extracts the subject and calls the `getMessageContent` method to retrieve the message content.
- The subject and message content are stored in a hash table (`subjectAndMessage`).
- The `getMessageContent` method checks if the message is a MIME message and retrieves its content.
- Finally, the `main` method demonstrates how to use the `receiveAndStoreMail` method with your IMAP server credentials.

Remember to replace the placeholder values (`imapServer`, `username`, `password`) with your actual IMAP server details and credentials. Also, ensure that you have the necessary JavaMail API libraries included in your project's classpath.

