Server-Sent Events (SSE) is a technology that allows a server to send event-driven updates to a client in real-time. It is a simple and efficient way to push data from the server to the client without the need for periodic requests. SSE is particularly useful for applications that require real-time updates, such as live notifications, stock tickers, or chat applications.

Here's an example of how you can implement Server-Sent Events using Express.js, a popular Node.js web framework:

**Server-Side (Node.js with Express.js):**
```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
const port = 3000;

app.use(express.static('public'));

app.get('/sse', (req, res) => {
    res.writeHead(200, {
        'Content-Type': 'text/event-stream',
        'Cache-Control': 'no-cache',
        'Connection': 'keep-alive'
    });

    let messageCount = 0;

    setInterval(() => {
        const message = `event: message\n${messageCount++}\n\n`;
        res.write(message);
    }, 2000); // Send a new message every 2 seconds
});

app.listen(port, () => {
    console.log(`SSE server listening on port ${port}`);
});
```

In this example, the Express.js app sets up a route at `/sse` that will handle SSE requests. When a client connects to this endpoint, the server sends a response with the appropriate headers to indicate that it's an SSE connection. The `Content-Type` header is set to `text/event-stream`, which is the standard format for SSE.

The server then uses a `setInterval` function to periodically generate new messages and send them to the client. Each message is wrapped in an event stream format, starting with the `event:` keyword, followed by the message content, and ending with a newline character.

**Client-Side (HTML and JavaScript):**
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>SSE Example</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Server-Sent Events Example</h1>
    <div id="messages"></div>

    <script>
        const messagesDiv = document.getElementById('messages');
        const xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();

        xhr.open('GET', 'http://localhost:3000/sse', true);
        xhr.onreadystatechange = function() {
            if (xhr.readyState === 4 && xhr.status === 200) {
                messagesDiv.innerHTML += xhr.responseText;
            }
        };
        xhr.send();

        setInterval(function() {
            xhr.send();
        }, 5000); // Fetch new data every 5 seconds
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

On the client-side, you can create an HTML file with a simple interface to display the messages. The JavaScript code sets up an XMLHttpRequest object to fetch data from the SSE endpoint. It reads the response text and appends it to the `messages` div. The `setInterval` function is used to periodically fetch new data, ensuring that the client stays connected to the server.

When you run this Express.js server and open the HTML file in a web browser, you'll see a simple interface that displays messages sent by the server in real-time. This is a basic example, but SSE can be used in various scenarios, providing a powerful way to enable real-time communication between the server and clients.

